\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The signature at constant flux}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{On one oriented spin copy, at #{aM=1} and constant scalar angle #{|\phi|<1/32} on the torus of \ref{fdip-019X}, the half-signature #{(\nu_{-}-\nu_{+})/2} is #{-w}, where #{w=N_1N_2\phi /(2\pi)}.}

\proof{\p{At zero curvature the unitary shifts commute. On each simultaneous character their regular Hermitian Pauli symbol is traceless, hence has one sign of each kind. Otherwise use \ref{fdip-01A3} and \ref{fdip-01A6} to work at the endpoint there. Let #{I} consist of angles with #{-\pi /2\le y<\pi /2} in the representative range #{-\pi\le y<\pi}. Assign every eigenangle to #{I} or its complement on the circle. In the complement pair the upper and lower #{E_y} by the identity. Within #{I}, whenever both #{y} and #{z=y+\phi} lie in #{I}, pair lower #{E_y} with upper #{E_z} by #{X}. These orthogonal pairs include every nonzero off-diagonal action: #{b(y)>0} requires #{y\notin I}, and #{t(z)>0} implies #{\cos z>1/4}, so #{z} is farther than #{1/32} from either endpoint of #{I} and #{z-\phi\in I}. All unpaired fibers carry only the indicated diagonal.}

\p{In the outer pair the two-dimensional scalar factor has determinant #{-\sin^2 y-b(y)^2<0}: at its only sine zero, #{\pi}, the second term is strictly negative. On an inner pair the corresponding determinant is #{-\sin y\sin z-t(z)^2}. If the sine product is positive this is negative. Otherwise the short interval from #{y} to #{z} contains zero, so #{|y|,|z|\le|\phi|}, #{t(z)>1/2}, and the absolute sine product is at most #{(1/32)^2}. Each pair therefore contributes equal dimensions of both signs.}

\p{For #{\phi>0}, a lower fiber left unpaired at #{y\in I} exits near #{+\pi /2}; its action #{\sin y} is positive. An upper fiber left unpaired at #{z\in I} enters near #{-\pi /2}; its action #{-\sin z} is also positive. For #{\phi<0} both signs reverse. Set #{V=N_1N_2}, #{g=\gcd(|w|,V)}, #{L=V/g} and #{j=|w|/g}. Each orbit of addition by #{\phi} is a translate of #{L} equally spaced angles, since #{j} and #{L} are coprime. Its fibers have a common dimension #{m_o} by unitarity of #{X}. For either collection of unpaired fibers the half-open interval has length #{2\pi j/L}, containing exactly #{j} orbit angles even when one lies on its endpoint. Since #{\sum_o Lm_o=V}, each collection has dimension #{jV/L=|w|}. Together they give half-signature #{-w}.}}
